\title{Three-Particle Azimuthal Correlations in $p+p$, $d+\mathrm{Au}$, and $\mathrm{Au}+\mathrm{Au}$ Collisions at $\sqrt{s_{\mathrm{NN}}} = 200$~GeV at STAR: Conical Flow vs. QCD-Cerenkov Radiation}

\author{The STAR Collaboration}
\date{\today}

\begin{abstract}
We present measurements of three-particle azimuthal correlations in $p+p$, $d+\mathrm{Au}$, and $\mathrm{Au}+\mathrm{Au}$ collisions at $\sqrt{s_{\mathrm{NN}}} = 200~\mathrm{GeV}$ recorded by the STAR detector at RHIC. The correlations are evaluated using high-transverse-momentum trigger particles ($3 < p_T^{\mathrm{trig}} < 4~\mathrm{GeV}/c$) associated with lower-$p_T$ particles ($1 < p_T^{\mathrm{assoc}} < 2~\mathrm{GeV}/c$) to probe the spatial structure of medium response to high-$p_T$ partonic energy loss. A novel three-particle correlation analysis technique is utilized to disentangle intrinsic jet topology modifications from the large background of combinatorial pairs and flow modulations. We rigorously address the systematic uncertainties associated with the background subtraction, focusing specifically on the impact of elliptic ($v_2$) and hexadecapole ($v_4$) anisotropic flow harmonics, as well as the effects of residual jet-flow couplings. In central $\mathrm{Au}+\mathrm{Au}$ collisions, a characteristic double-peaked (off-axis) correlation structure is observed on the away-side ($\Delta\phi \sim \pi$). We compare the angular parameters of this structure across the different collision systems ($p+p$, $d+\mathrm{Au}$, and centrality-dependent $\mathrm{Au}+\mathrm{Au}$) to evaluate candidate mechanisms for medium response, specifically focusing on hydrodynamic conical flow (Mach cone shockwaves) versus particle emission induced by QCD-Cerenkov radiation. 
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\end{document}
